course: biology
lesson: 160
title: The Congo river: tigerfish, the deepest river, lungfish and hidden birds
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Congo is the deepest river known: in places it plunges more than two hundred metres, deeper than a tall tower, cut by fast water over a very long time.
explain: In its roaring rapids the goliath tigerfish hunts with long dagger teeth that fit together like a trap, and it can grow as big as a large child.
explain: In the murky water an elephantnose fish cannot see, so it makes weak electricity and feels the world by its electric field, and in dried-up pools a lungfish sleeps in a ball of mud until the rain comes back.
explain: Cartoon hippos like Gloria in Madagascar swim and dance; real hippos cannot really swim, so they walk and bounce along the bottom and come up to breathe.
fact: The goliath tigerfish is one of the fiercest river fish, with long teeth that stick out like a crocodile's, and hunts in the fast rough water of the Congo.
fact: Parts of the Congo River are more than two hundred metres deep, the deepest river known.
fact: Some fish in the deep rapids are cut off by strong currents deep down in the dark, where eyes and colour matter little, and have become blind and pale.
fact: The African dwarf crocodile is one of the smallest crocodiles, usually under two metres, and hunts fish and frogs at night in forest streams.
fact: In some caves dwarf crocodiles have turned orange, possibly from living among bat droppings.
fact: Hippos cannot really swim; they are so heavy they sink, so they walk or bounce along the bottom with ears and nostrils closed, and come up to breathe.
fact: Hippos make a red oily fluid on their skin that works like sunscreen and helps stop germs, often called blood sweat, though it is not blood or sweat.
fact: Elephantnose fish make weak electricity to sense their surroundings and talk to each other in dark, muddy water.
fact: African lungfish dig into mud when the water dries up, wrap themselves in a slime cocoon and breathe air through a small hole until the rains come, sometimes for years.
fact: The Congo peafowl, a shy cousin of the Indian peacock, lives only in the Congo forest; a feather in a villager's hat puzzled a scientist years before the bird was named in 1936.
fact: The African forest buffalo is a smaller, reddish cousin of the savanna buffalo, with swept-back horns that fit dense forest.
fact: The hammer-headed bat is the largest bat in Africa, and males have huge heads to make loud honking calls that females listen to.
fact: The Congo River has more than seven hundred kinds of fish, many found nowhere else, and new kinds are still described almost every year.
fact: African pygmy kingfishers are tiny kingfishers that mostly hunt insects in woods, not fish in rivers.
exercise: What makes the goliath tigerfish fearsome? | Long dagger teeth that fit together like a trap | Poisonous spines on its back | Electric shocks | Its huge size, as big as a whale, so it can swallow boats
exercise: Where does the goliath tigerfish hunt? | In the fast, rough water of the Congo River | In the calm, shallow sea near the river mouth | In tiny puddles on the forest floor after the rain | In dark underground caves far from any river
exercise: How deep is the Congo River in places? | More than two hundred metres | About two metres | About twenty metres | More than two kilometres
exercise: Why are some fish in the Congo rapids blind and pale? | Strong currents cut them off deep down, where eyes and colour matter little | They are old and sick | They live up in the bright sunlight near the surface, so they have no need to hide | Tigerfish bit their eyes
exercise: How big is the African dwarf crocodile usually? | Under two metres | About six metres long | About twenty centimetres | About ten metres long
exercise: Why do hippos walk along the riverbed? | They are so heavy that they sink and cannot really swim | They are looking for fish | They like the cold mud | They are afraid of the surface, where birds could catch them
exercise: What is a hippo's «blood sweat»? | A red oily fluid that works like sunscreen and stops germs | Real blood from cuts | Ordinary sweat like ours | Mud from the river bank that has dried and turned red in the hot sun
exercise: How does an elephantnose fish find its way in muddy water? | It makes weak electricity and senses the electric field | It sees in the dark with huge eyes | It follows the tigerfish | It smells everything around it with a very long nose, even in the mud
exercise: What does a lungfish do when its pool dries up? | Digs into the mud, wraps in slime and breathes air until the rain | Walks overland all the way to the sea on its strong front fins | Dies straight away | Flies to a new pool
exercise: How long can a lungfish survive in dried mud? | Sometimes for years | About an hour | About a day | Only one night
exercise: What is the Congo peafowl? | A shy cousin of the Indian peacock living only in the Congo forest | A kind of parrot | A big wild chicken that escaped from the farms in the villages long ago | A kind of duck from the river
exercise: What first puzzled a scientist about the Congo peafowl? | A strange feather in a villager's hat | A photograph on a postcard | A song on the radio | An egg in a museum
exercise: How is the forest buffalo different from the savanna buffalo? | Smaller and reddish with swept-back horns | Bigger and black, with huge curly horns like a ram | It has no horns at all, only two small bumps | It has black and white stripes like a zebra
exercise: Why does a male hammer-headed bat have such a big head? | To make loud honking calls that females listen to | To break open hard nuts and fruit | To hammer insects | To store water
exercise: About how many kinds of fish live in the Congo River? | More than seven hundred | About seven | About seventy | About seventy thousand
exercise: What does an African pygmy kingfisher mostly eat? | Insects caught in woods | Big fish caught in the river | Seeds and fruit from the trees | Frogs from the deep river
test: In a cartoon, Gloria the hippo swims gracefully in a pool. What is true of real hippos? | They cannot really swim; they walk and bounce along the bottom | They swim like dolphins | They never go into water | They float on top of the water all day long, like big grey logs
test: «that hippo's bleeding all over, is it hurt??» What is the best answer? | Probably not; it makes a red oily fluid that works like sunscreen | Yes, hippos always bleed through their skin when they stay out in the sun too long | Yes, a crocodile bit it | It is red mud only
test: A fisherman shows a fish with big teeth and says it is part shark. What is it most likely? | A goliath tigerfish, which only looks sharky | A real shark that swam up into the river from the sea | A mix of a shark and a pike, born in the river | A baby whale that lost its way far up the river
test: A tigerfish leaps at a bird skimming over the water. Why can that work? | It hunts fast and has long teeth that trap prey | It can leap out and fly for a few metres above the water | It shoots a jet of water at the bird to knock it down | It is not a fish at all but a bird that dives into the river
test: 3 hippos walk along the riverbed. How many ears and how many tails? | 6 ears and 3 tails | 3 ears and 6 tails | 12 ears and 3 tails | 6 ears and 6 tails
test: A dwarf crocodile and 2 forest buffalo stand near a swamp. How many legs and horns? | 12 legs and 4 horns | 8 legs and 2 horns | 12 legs and 6 horns | 10 legs and 4 horns
test: «found a mud ball, gonna smash it» Why might that be a bad idea in the Congo? | It could hold a lungfish waiting for the rain | Mud balls explode | It might be a bird's nest made of gold, hidden there long ago | Mud is poisonous to touch
test: What would happen if the elephantnose fish's electricity stopped working? | It could not sense its way or other fish in the dark water | It would see better | It would see much better without the electric field to confuse it | Nothing would change
test: «kingfishers eat fish, it's in the name, innit» What is right? | Not always; the pygmy kingfisher mostly snatches insects | Yes, every kingfisher in the world eats only fish and nothing else | Kingfishers eat only seeds | Kingfishers are a kind of fish
test: «why's no one ever seen that peacock thing» Why is the Congo peafowl so rarely seen? | It is shy and lives only deep in the Congo forest | It only comes out once a year | It is extinct | It lives underground in burrows and only comes up at night to feed
test: A loud honking fills the forest at night. What is a likely source? | Male hammer-headed bats calling to females | A hippo snoring | Leopards purring | A lungfish in its mud ball
test: Why can scientists not reach the bottom of the deepest parts of the Congo with a pole? | The river is more than two hundred metres deep there | The bottom of the river is made of soft mud that swallows the poles | Tigerfish bite the poles | The water flows uphill
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the depth of the Congo River. | Certain sections exceed two hundred metres in depth, rendering it the deepest river known | Its depth does not exceed two metres at any point | It is the shallowest of the great rivers of Africa | Its depth has been established as approximately two kilometres, exceeding that of most seas
exam: Identify the principal means by which the elephantnose fish perceives its surroundings in turbid water. | The generation of weak electric fields and the detection of their alteration | Vision of exceptional acuity in darkness | The emission of high-pitched calls and the detection of their echoes from the riverbed | Contact with the riverbed by means of its fins
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of the African lungfish during drought? | It encases itself in mud and a cocoon of slime and breathes air until the return of rain | It migrates overland to the nearest permanent river, travelling by night upon its fins | It perishes upon the evaporation of the water | It ascends into the trees until the rains resume
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the hippopotamus progresses along the riverbed rather than swimming. | Its great weight causes it to sink, and it is unable to swim in the proper sense | It pursues fish upon the riverbed | It avoids the warmth of the surface water and remains in the cool mud at all times | It is pursued upon the surface by crocodiles
